\label{fig:figure_forecasts_plot1}
% \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, inner]{overleaf-logo}
<<forecast_models_plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE>>=
tar_read(plot_models_1, store="../_targets")
@
\caption{U.S. national incident hospitalization forecasts during the testing phase, faceted by model}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Overall model performance} \label{testing_overall}
Both location scales show the same model ranking for the overall testing phase evaluation, with the top five models outperforming the COVIDhub-baseline. The COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble takes the lead for best WIS and MAE by a significant margin. The next best three models---THieF\_6wk-4root, THieF\_ensemble-train3, THieF\_ensemble-mean---all have fairly similar relative WIS and relative MAE values, as do the two worst-performing models: THieF\_12wk-noTransform and sarima\_s7-noTransform. However, there are some notable differences between performance for averaged states versus U.S. national.

For averaged states (see Table \ref{tab:table_overall_states}), the WIS and MAE values are more closely clustered together and are of a smaller magnitude. Every best subset model is well-calibrated, with near-nominal coverage rates at both uncertainty levels. In fact, our models had better 50\% coverage rates than those of the Forecast Hub models. In addition, for this location scale the models in the lower half of the ranking, including the COVIDhub-baseline, all showed very similar relative WIS and relative MAE values.

For U.S. national (see Table \ref{tab:table_overall_US}), however, the WIS and MAE values tend to be more spread out, partially a result of their larger magnitude. At this location scale, our models had below-nominal coverage rates for both uncertainty levels. The Forecast Hub models had above-nominal coverage rates at the 50\% level but also fell short of nominal coverage at the 95\% level. The THieF\_6wk-noTransform model showed more similar performance to the top four ranked models compared to that of the COVIDhub-baseline, unlike for averaged states. The bottom two models for the U.S. national ranking were also substantially worse compared to the COVIDhub-baseline in terms of relative WIS and relative MAE values.

