\subsection{Model performance by horizon week} \label{testing_horizon}
Only WIS plots are shown because this is our principle evaluation metric and the MAE plots are very similar.

The by-horizon evaluation shows similar model rankings to those of the overall evaluation (see Figure \ref{fig:figure_horizon_plot} and Supplemental Tables \ref{tab:table_horizon_states} and \ref{tab:table_horizon_us}). The top four models for both location scales are the same and perform no worse (but usually tend to do better) than the COVIDhub-baseline at all horizons, and the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble significantly outperforms all other seven models.  However, aggregating the forecasts by horizon illustrates some important differences in accuracy between horizon weeks.

For averaged states (see Figure \ref{fig:figure_horizon_plot}), THieF\_6wk-4root outperforms the THieF ensembles for WIS end MAE at all horizons, except for 1-week ahead. Similarly, THieF\_6wk-noTransform has lower WIS and MAE than three models at 2- to 4-weeks ahead but is only better than THieF\_12wk-noTransform for the 1-week horizon. Additionally, while sarima\_s7-noTransform is ranked fifth and THieF\_12-noTransform is ranked last for the 1-week ahead horizon, these ranks flip to be sixth and last for the 3- and 4-week ahead horizons, after briefly crossing with each other (and the COVIDhub-baseline) at 2-weeks ahead. The COVIDhub-baseline is consistently the second worst model. Coverage rates remain high across horizons for all models, with 95\% coverage rates fall into no more than 10% of the nominal rate at any horizon.

For U.S. national (see Figure \ref{fig:figure_horizon_plot}) WIS and MAE, THieF\_6wk-4root outperforms the THieF ensembles for the two longest horizons while the ensembles (and COVIDhub-baseline) perform slightly better at the shortest horizon. The difference in performance among the three models is barely discernible at a horizon of 2 weeks. The sarima\_s7-noTransform and THieF\_12wk-noTransform models occupy the bottom of the ranking, with THieF\_12wk-noTransform scoring better WIS values for all but the 1-week ahead horizon. The difference is especially pronounced at longer horizons, where this model has much lower WIS than that of sarima\_s7-noTransform. The COVIDhub-baseline ranks sixth for WIS at the U.S. national location scale for all but the 1-week ahead horizon where it ranks fourth. Coverage rates are slightly lower at this location scale, falling to up to 19\% below the nominal rate (though most are no lower than 15\%).

\begin{figure}
\label{fig:figure_horizon_plot}
% \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, inner]{overleaf-logo}
<<horizon_plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE>>=
tar_read(combined_wis_plot, store="../_targets")
@
\caption{Average WIS plotted by horizon week during the testing phase for each model at both location scales}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Model performance by pandemic wave} \label{testing_wave}
One overall theme that continues for the by--pandemic wave evaluation (see Figure \ref{fig:figure_wave_plot} and Supplemental Tables \ref{tab:table_wave_states} and \ref{tab:table_wave_states}) is that the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble ranks first for lowest WIS, and this model is substantially better than all the other models at every horizon. For each pandemic wave--location scale combination, most horizons show the next three models to be THieF\_6wk-4root and the two THieF ensembles, with THieF\_12wk-4root and sarima\_s7-noTransform ranking near the bottom. The COVIDhub-baseline also ranks similarly low among the eight models. Plots for the same pandemic wave generally bear greater resemblance in terms of overall trends than plots for the same location scale; the one notable exception is numerical scale for average WIS.

For the Omicron variant wave, the best and worst models---COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble and sarima\_s7-noTransform---are easily distinguishable from the middle six models. THieF\_ensemble-mean and THieF\_ensemble-train3 rank second and third for short horizons but drop to forth and fifth for the 4-week ahead horizon. THieF\_6wk-4root performs better at all horizons for the U.S. national scale during this wave. The COVIDhub-baseline also appears to perform better for U.S. national. On the other hand, sarima\_s7-noTransform has more comparable performance for the averaged states scale at every horizon. All models have greater WIS values during the Omicron wave compared to during the BA.4/BA.5 wave for their respective location scale.

For the BA.4/BA.5 variant wave, model rankings by WIS are more distinct and tend to remain static from one horizon week to another. The best three and worst models are the same regardless of location scale; however, the COVIDhub-baseline is noticeably better ranked for U.S. national scores, where it is approximately tied with THieF\_ensemble-mean for fourth-best, compared to a clear seventh place for averaged states scores. THieF\_6wk-noTransform also performs as well or better than sarima\_s7-noTransform for all horizons during this wave at the U.S. national scale, while the ranks of these two models depends on horizon length at the averaged states scale. Coverage rates are exceedingly high for the averaged states level and approximately nominal for all but the last-ranked models for U.S. national.

\begin{figure}
\label{fig:figure_wave_plot}
% \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, inner]{overleaf-logo}
<<wave_plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE>>=
tar_read(combined_wis_wave_plot, store="../_targets")
@
\caption{Average WIS plotted by pandemic wave and horizon week during the testing phase for each model at both location scales}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Model performance by forecast week} \label{testing_forecast_week}
For the by--forecast week evaluation, we make four plots for each location scale (see Figures \ref{fig:figure_date_plot_states} and \ref{fig:figure_date_plot_us}), with WIS or 95\% PI coverage (restricted to either 1-week or 4-week ahead) on the y-axis and forecast date on the x-axis. The WIS plots generally follow the shape of the incident hospitalization truth data (see Figure \ref{fig:figure_evaluate_period_plot}), with greater WIS observed during times of high incidence and rapid change. The 95\% coverage plots tend to follow a vertically flipped transformation of the truth data, with below-nominal coverage rates observed during times of high incidence and rapid change.

Many of the patterns observed in the evaluations described above continued to hold for this subset of the testing phase analysis. The COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble consistently is one of the the top few, if not the top, models for both WIS and 95\% coverage. The one exception is near the crest of the Omicron wave when this model ranks around seventh for lowest WIS at the 4-week ahead horizon. Here, and in a few other places, the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble loses out to THieF\_6wk-4root at the 4-week ahead horizon. The two THieF ensembles also rarely beat the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble in terms of WIS yet demonstrate incredibly stable performance, never ranking below sixth. In this way, the THieF ensembles are quite similar to the COVIDhub-baseline during times of high incidence and rapid change: their WIS is much more stable and consistent from week-to-week than that of other models; however, THieF\_ensemble-mean and THieF\_ensemble-train3 often out-perform the COVIDhub-baseline, which has the worst WIS for several weeks. Generally, though, THieF\_12wk-noTransform and sarima\_s7-noTransform alternate for having the worst WIS and 95\% coverage rates, the THieF model ranking better for 4-week metrics and sarima\_s7-noTransform for 1-week.

The by--forecast week break down of THieF\_6wk-noTransform is particularly interesting. This model tended to fall in the middle of the rankings for the previously described evaluations, yet Figures \ref{fig:figure_date_plot_states} and \ref{fig:figure_date_plot_us} show that this model actually tended to have more extreme performance, either performing rather well or quite badly. Investigating less aggregate scores for THieF\_12wk-noTransform similarly reveals several instances of good performance, usually experienced at long horizons under settings of rapid change or high incidence. In addition, we notice that sarima\_s7-noTransform demonstrated noticeably worse performance for U.S. national WIS at a horizon of 1 week compared to that for averaged states. Further, THieF\_6wk-4root's strangely low coverage for averaged states following the decline of the Omicron wave at both plotted horizons is also noteworthy, especially since the other seven models experience significantly higher coverage rates during this time.\footnote{Comparisons among models for U.S. national 95\% coverage by forecast week are harder to make since this location scale only includes seven 95\% interval forecasts for one location (compared to seven per 52 locations for averaged states), so we simply allow the plots in Figure \ref{fig:figure_date_plot_states} to speak for themselves.}

\begin{figure}
\label{fig:figure_date_plot_states}
% \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, inner]{overleaf-logo}
<<states_date_plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE>>=
tar_read(combined_wis_cov95_plot_states, store="../_targets")
@
\caption{Average h-week ahead WIS and 95\% PI coverage plotted by forecast week during the testing phase for each model for averaged states}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
\label{fig:figure_date_plot_us}
% \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, inner]{overleaf-logo}
<<us_date_plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE>>=
tar_read(combined_wis_cov95_plot_us, store="../_targets")
@
\caption{Average h-week ahead WIS and 95\% PI coverage plotted by forecast week during the testing phase for each model for U.S. national}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Model performance by location} \label{testing_location}
Figure \ref{fig:figure_location_plot} summarizes the by-location evaluation, with the models on the x-axis ordered by lowest-to-highest overall relative WIS at the average states location scale for the testing phase and locations on the y-axis ordered by descending cumulative hospitalizations. The top five models show either better or equal performance than the COVIDhub-baseline---represented by shades of blue ranging from dark to light and white---with the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble clearly the best. The next three models are fairly close in terms of lowest WIS across all of the locations, though THieF\_6wk-4root is marginally better with lower relative WIS by a difference of more than 0.05 in over 18% of locations. The worst two models include some shades of red which represent performance worse than the COVIDhub-baseline. However, it should be noted that these models actually perform better than the baseline for a significant number of locations, with THieF\_12wk-noTransform sharing the top rank for Puerto Rico and Alaska. In addition, most of the worst performance relative to the baseline occurs in the locations with the highest cumulative hospitalizations during the testing phase. This is perhaps unsurprising since we know that high-count settings tend to be more difficult to forecast accurately.

\begin{figure}
\label{fig:figure_location_plot}
% \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth, inner]{overleaf-logo}
<<location_plot, echo=FALSE, fig=TRUE>>=
tar_read(wis_location_plot, store="../_targets")
@
\caption{Relative WIS plotted by location during the testing phase for each model across all horizons}
\end{figure}


\chapter{Discussion} \label{discussion}
THieF is a unique forecast methodology that combines hierarchical forecasting with forecast combination that shows a number of improvements in forecast accuracy over the COVIDhub-baseline. Such accuracy gains vary based on each model's temporal hierarchy, with well-crafted THieF models demonstrating competitive performance. Different aggregation levels cause a model to forecast well under different settings, and we may take advantage of these diverse strengths by ensembling a range of THieF models. These THieF ensembles performed nearly as well as the best original THieF model, and all three best models produced consistently accurate forecasts for incident hospitalizations using real-time truth data during the 48-week long testing phase. Specifically, THieF\_6wk-4root almost always ranked second in terms of WIS and MAE. The testing phase evaluations allow us to draw the following conclusions about the models we generated and the underlying methodology.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The THieF ensembles and original THieF models with mid-length top aggregation levels are accurate compared to baseline model.} In terms of WIS and MAE, THieF\_6wk-4root, THieF\_ensemble-train3, and THieF\_ensemble-mean almost always beat the COVIDhub-baseline (performing equally well), while THieF\_6wk-noTransform almost always performed as well (though sometimes better) than the baseline. This was true across horizon, pandemic wave, forecast date, and location; for the top three models, approximately equal improvements occurred in stable, low incidence settings and rapidly-changing, high-incidence settings also showed moderate improvements. These models also demonstrated good 50\% coverage and reasonable 95\% coverage, with narrower (and thus typically more useful) prediction intervals. Hence, we conclude that these four models may be presented as better alternatives to the COVIDHub-baseline for incident hospitalizations.

\item \textbf{THieF\_6wk-4root, followed closely by the THieF ensembles, yields the best performance when compared to alternate models from the best subset.} THieF\_6wk-4root, THieF\_ensemble-train3, and THieF\_ensemble-mean tended to rank within the top four for every testing phase evaluation, which compared eight models including the COVIDhub-baseline and COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble. In fact, except for the 1-week ahead horizon during times of rapid change or high incidence, THieF\_6wk-4root ranked second for most analyses. While any of these models rarely beat the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble, these models consistently had competitive performance for all four metrics out of the best subset models that moved on from the validation phase.

\item \textbf{THieF\_6wk-4root, followed closely by the THieF ensembles, yields the best performance when compared to alternate models from the best subset.} THieF\_6wk-4root, THieF\_ensemble-train3, and THieF\_ensemble-mean tended to rank within the top four for every testing phase evaluation, which compared eight models including the COVIDhub-baseline and COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble. In fact, except for the 1-week ahead horizon during times of rapid change or high incidence, THieF\_6wk-4root ranked second for most analyses. It should be noted, however, that despite slightly lower rankings, especially during times of high incidence or rapid change, the THieF ensembles showed greater stability and resistance to jumps in WIS, MAE, and PI coverage compared to THieF\_6wk-4root. Overall, these models consistently had competitive performance for all four metrics out of the best subset models that moved on from the validation phase; therefore, we conclude that any of these three models would be suitable for informing decision-making related to COVID-19.

\item \textbf{There may not be a meaningful difference between the trained and untrained THieF ensembles.} THieF\_ensemble-train3 and THieF\_ensemble-mean showed limited differences in performance during the testing phase analysis, regardless of location scale, horizon, pandemic wave, forecast date, or evaluation metric. THieF\_ensemble-train3 is marginally better than the untrained THieF\_ensemble-mean in most evaluations, the largest difference in relative WIS being 0.07 for any single horizon, horizon--wave, or location comparison between the models. Usually, though, the relative WIS of these two models is much closer. The by--forecast date plots provide further evidence of their similarity, as the two ensembles have almost identical WIS for every forecast date (see Figures \ref{fig:figure_date_plot_states} and \ref{fig:figure_date_plot_us}). Since this trained ensemble has a small $\theta$ value, indicating a lighter weighting scheme, this result is not all too surprising.

\item \textbf{The accuracy improvements of the original THieF models with mid-length top aggregation levels over the baseline are likely a result of the THieF methodology itself, not the underlying ARIMA base forecaster.} During the analyses of both phases, sarima\_s7-noTransform demonstrated poor performance, as did the other SARIMA models in the validation phase, and only rarely beat the COVIDhub-baseline. The best performance sarima\_s7-noTransform achieved occurred during static, low-count periods at short horizons, which was equivalent to that of higher ranking models. We thus conclude that it does not the underlying ARIMA base forecaster that lends most THieF models their accuracy improvements but rather the other aspects of the THieF methodology. Namely, the union of hierarchical forecasting and forecast combination.

\item \textbf{Choosing the correct top aggregation level\footnote{and perhaps all other aggregation levels} is essential to achieving the best results when using the THieF methodology.} We saw a range of performance demonstrated by the different THieF models during the validation and testing phase analyses, with THieF\_6wk-4root consistently obtaining some of the best WIS, followed by THieF\_6wk-noTransform. Conversely, THieF\_12wk-noTransform nearly always ranked near the bottom for all evaluations. Other THieF models also out-performed the COVIDhub-baseline during the validation phase, including the THieF models with the second longest top aggregation level of eight weeks. Hence, it appears that any accuracy gains from THieF methodology are dependent on the choice of top aggregation level.

% \item \textbf{The THieF ensembles combine the best aspects of each component model to produce a more accurate model overall.} The THieF ensembles consist of fourteen (14) original THieF component models with top aggregation levels of periods ranging from 1 to 12 weeks. THieF\_12wk-noTransform, while generally one of the worst models for all metrics, performs competitively under high-count or rapidly-changing settings, especially for long horizons. Conversely, sarima\_s7-noTransform (which shares some important similarities with THieF\_1wk-noTransform) showcases similarly competitive performance during low-count or stable settings while otherwise occupying the bottom ranks. The THieF ensembles are the best models overall, sans the COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble, and perform fairly well under all settings. Therefore, based on our results and other prior research\cite{ensemble_accuracy}, we conclude that the accuracy gains experienced by THieF ensembles originate from the strengths of their component models.
\end{itemize}

We have demonstrated that THieF is a powerful methodology that can model the real-world complexities of an infectious disease system, provided that appropriate top aggregation levels are chosen for the temporal hierarchy. The initial base forecasts are created using a classic statistical model, a type of model that has shown good performance when forecasting outbreak settings\cite{lauer}, but it is the final reconciliation of these forecasts that likely explains THieF's accuracy gains. Ensembling a large number of THieF models with a range of top aggregation levels significantly added to initial accuracy improvements for all but the best original THieF model. Finally, careful selection of the period of analysis and day of the week on which to make forecasts helped to prevent any potential issues due to missing data, while the choice of incident hospitalizations as the forecast target limited in accuracies due to under reporting.

%While we chose an underlying ARIMA model to implement THieF (based on recommendations from Athanasoupolos), there are a multitude of alternative classic statistical models that

Further work might consider different evaluation metrics, alternative ways of aggregating forecast scores, or implementing logarithmic scores. The magnitude of WIS and MAE is dependent on the scale of forecasts, meaning that averages calculated from these metrics will be dominated by forecasts with large WIS and MAE values. For example, average metrics calculated during the entire validation phase will have the largest contributions from longer horizons and high-count locations; that is, a model that forecasts low-count locations well but high-count locations poorly is penalized in the overall evaluation more harshly than that of a model that does the opposite. The same is true for short versus long horizons. We attempted to address this issue by considering a range of stratified analyses, but these evaluations are limited in scope. Future work that makes use of logarithmic scores may better solve the problem of unequal contributions to metric values.

The other main direction for future work is employing one of the existing probabilistic applications of THieF to forecast COVID-19 incident hospitalizations or other outbreak data. Such a method would generate a complete probabilistic distribution instead of using prediction intervals from point forecasts, which may be more useful or more accurate. Alternative ensemble formulations might also be considered, such as a median ensemble, a trained ensemble with a shifting $\theta$ value, or even ensembles of THieF implementations specifically tailored for point and probabilistic forecasting. We are also curious about accuracy comparisons to other models that submitted to the Forecast Hub during the period of analysis.

We demonstrated the utility and success of THieF and ensembles of THieF models when forecasting incident hospitalizations for COVID-19 through the results of this paper. THieF models with well-selected top aggregation levels and THieF ensembles demonstrate clear improvements over the baseline model for both point and probabilistic forecasts, with THieF\_6wk-4root ranking just below the top-performing COVIDhub-4\_week\_ensemble model. Infections disease forecasting continues to be important for acute disease outbreaks, epidemics, pandemic, and endemic diseases, and we hope that the THieF methodology will be used in such settings given its demonstrated accuracy improvements in this application.


